WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.620
Film Courage: So before the interview we were
taking a look at your website and it’s excellent.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.950
It’s Actfourscreenplays.com.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.500
And you have so many interesting parts with
it, so we’re going to have some questions

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.559
with that.

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:15.820
One of which is I think something you wrote
where you put “In Hollywood, nothing will

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.710
ever be stronger than a great story.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:23.730
Trends come and go, stars rise and fall, and
studios succeed and fold, but a great story

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.950
will always be king.”

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:31.350
I’m wondering if you can talk about why
story is king and some of the parts to that.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.220
Daniel Calvisi: Well, story is timeless.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:41.050
A great script or a great piece of material
is timeless.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:45.600
Trends do come and go in film.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:54.770
Visuals and use of CGI, development with digital
equipment.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.630
You know all of these things, 3d versus 2d,
now we’re getting into virtual reality.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.700
But what really matters is that great story.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.320
It doesn’t matter how big the budget of
the movie is.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.510
It doesn’t matter if it’s in 3D.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.690
The audience is going to respond if it’s
a great movie and a great story.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:19.640
So a great piece of material can really blow
away everything.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.870
Think about an actor, they are going to go
through various levels of popularity.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:27.180
They may be hot right now or not.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.360
But a great script is always going to be a
great script.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.450
So that’s really the only currency in Hollywood
that really means something.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:39.850
“No one knows anything,” as William Goldwyn
famously said about Hollywood.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.840
And look, there have been a number of movies
in Hollywood this year (as with any year)

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.640
that bomb and probably a big reason is the
material.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.640
It’s not just that the stars are hot this
year.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.020
PASSENGERS with basically the two hottest
stars in the world (Chris Pratt and Jennifer

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.010
Lawrence) came out and it totally bombed.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:07.180
And I didn’t see it but I heard from some
people who did there is a lot wrong with apparently

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.620
the script, the structure, with just the story
in general.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.450
The story didn’t grab people and it didn’t
matter how big the stars were in that role.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.660
So it’s really the only currency that you
have for writers.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.920
Your only currency is your material.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:31.930
It’s your spec script, whether it’s a
spec pilot for a TV series or a spec feature.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:36.640
Film Courage: I find that so interesting that
yes story is king, but the writer gets so

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.580
little credit and is often not even brought
in on set and so many changes are made unbeknownst

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:43.580
to them.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.920
So I find that fascinating because it really
does come down to that story.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.510
Because you talked about being a script reader
and there were a few stories that you still

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.530
remember to this day.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:59.720
And how whether they pulled at your own heartstrings
or whatever it was, that story really does

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:00.720
stay with you.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.780
Can you think of some films that you know
even from a child that really stayed with

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.700
you and maybe they didn’t have huge stars
attached, maybe they did?

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:16.290
Daniel Calvisi: Well, I just ordered two movies
on BluRay that I hadn’t had on BluRay that

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:22.700
I just ordered on Amazon and they are MINORITY
REPORT and THE WIZARD OF OZ.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.290
THE WIZARD OF OZ is one of my favorite movies
of all time, it’s a classic.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:35.000
But I primarily got it because I wanted to
show it to my nieces and nephews…some day…if

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.750
we’re all there for Christmas or something.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.480
I want to make sure that they see it.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.129
They probably will see it at some point.

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:45.390
But just in case they hadn’t seen it or
if they have and it’s Christmas and we’re

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.010
looking for something family friendly and
I wanted it on BluRay and I wanted it in the

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.300
best quality possible and it’s timeless.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.510
It’s never going to not be great.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:58.140
So right there it’s a generational thing.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.810
I want to pass this on to the next generation.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:06.690
Now I don’t know how old they have to be
before I can show them GOODFELLAS or if they

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:13.090
will ever like GOODFELLAS, but I would love
to see if they like GOODFELLAS or RAIDERS

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.090
OF THE LOST ARK.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.209
Film Courage: Sure.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:16.669
We will wait until college to show them.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:17.539
Daniel Calvisi: Yeah..maybe college.

